WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.946
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:11.040
Something that is
useful to know about MIT

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.630
is that it's like
drinking from a fire hose.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:19.230
They will often tell you about
this in tours and everything,

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:22.380
too, but you really get to
experience it when you first

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.095
start your classes.

00:00:24.095 --> 00:00:25.470
So there's lots
of great research

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:28.800
to do at MIT, a lot of great
classes to take as well.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.080
But I think what
maybe some people

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.620
don't know about is that there's
lots of really cool activities

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:35.760
you can get involved with.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:36.930
There's a sailing club.

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:38.400
You can do rowing.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.150
There's any number of
different activities

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:44.160
you can get involved
with while you're here.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.590
I think people should know
that MIT has a lot to offer.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:50.310
Which means that
you'll have lots

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:53.910
of classes, lots of
extracurriculars,

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.627
lots of sports options.

00:00:55.627 --> 00:00:57.210
And so that means
that, basically, you

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:00.300
can be overwhelmed with the
number of things going on,

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.550
and just to pick and choose.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:06.030
Drink them all in, but
be choosy about what

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:08.460
you should do with your time
and what you should work on.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:09.835
The people here
are really great.

00:01:09.835 --> 00:01:12.420
And I think that the people are
what makes this place special.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:15.930
So in my experience,
not only are people here

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:17.400
obviously really
smart, but I think

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.110
they're also super
down to earth,

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.960
very easy to talk to
and have conversations

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:25.620
with-- not just about academics,
but also stuff outside of that.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.810
And so I found
that it's actually

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:30.840
been a surprisingly
balanced place in terms

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.840
of, yeah, just interests.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.660
And also I think
that a lot of people

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:39.315
here, because they are so
passionate about what they

00:01:39.315 --> 00:01:40.690
do and love what
they do so much,

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:42.390
they're always happy
to talk about it

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:44.430
and want to have
conversations about it.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:47.880
So I think people may
sometimes be intimidated.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.400
But I would say, yeah,
don't be intimidated

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:51.990
in talking to people.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:55.200
And everyone I think
is, in general,

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.640
quite welcoming around here.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.430
Well, MIT is a super
collaborative environment.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:03.750
So do not hesitate to reach
out to people around you

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:09.690
and explore any potential future
projects that you could work on

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:10.440
together.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.830
Because everyone has
a unique expertise.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:17.670
So by working together, you
can achieve greater things.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.370
So I think that there are
things that I could say related

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:25.252
to your coursework or to
the intellectual inquiry

00:02:25.252 --> 00:02:27.210
that you'll do and how
to think about research.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.580
But I think what maybe
is more important

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:32.310
than any of that to
your long-term success

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:36.390
in the program is
finding a group of people

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:37.530
that you resonate with.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:41.130
And I think sometimes
people come into the program

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:43.950
and they get in a local minima,
where they're surrounded

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:45.780
by a certain type
of people, and they

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:48.630
think that this is true for
the rest of the community.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.010
And that's not the case.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:53.220
And you can find people
that will resonate with you

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:55.650
and make you feel like
you're growing and learning

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:58.213
in the right ways and make
you feel like you belong.

00:02:58.213 --> 00:02:59.880
And so if you're
interacting with people

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:01.740
that don't make you
feel like you belong,

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:03.630
there's someone else
that you can work with.

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:06.780
I think it's really, really
important that you know

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:08.250
how to take care of yourself.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:12.300
So if you come to MIT, you
need to find a support network.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.100
And you need to rely
on those people.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.610
Because it's going to be
hard if you come here.

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:19.650
That's the nature of the
work that we're doing,

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.450
and it's the nature
of the Institute.

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.820
And so finding those
friends, finding

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:27.930
those people you can rely on,
that will get you through.

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:30.740
It's cold, but you
can deal with it.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:32.640
It takes a little bit
of suffering tolerance.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:35.690
[MUSIC PLAYING]

